WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.019
Film Courage: Do you remember something that
was said to you in one of these UCLA writing

00:00:03.019 --> 00:00:05.250
classes which you never forgot?

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.630
Patricia Vidal Delgado, Writer/Director: Yes,
definitely.

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:09.290
Richard Walter [recently retired Professor
and Associate and Interim Dean of the UCLA

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:15.830
School of Theater, Film and Television for
40 years] said nobody wants to see the film

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:18.450
of the village of the nice happy people.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:24.480
I remember that one because it’s so true.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:35.650
And also he said something which is in Hollywood
all you do is graduate to the next level disappointment.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:42.070
I think he used the example that you write
the script but it doesn’t sell.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:44.431
It’s shot but it’s not distributed.

00:00:44.431 --> 00:00:50.330
It’s distributed but not in great venues.

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:59.300
So you basically just graduate to the next
level which is very grounding advice that

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:04.989
you are always going to be walking the tightrope
in this industry no matter how well you think

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:07.490
you’re doing you are going to be walking
a tightrope.

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:10.490
Film Courage: Very interesting.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:18.069
Also how we were saying the European Mindset
of “Just try it and see what happens.”

00:01:18.069 --> 00:01:22.329
Do you think that coupled with that piece
of advice has helped you to be like “You

00:01:22.329 --> 00:01:27.950
know what, I’ll try it and submit to Sundance.”

00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:29.969
For someone’s first feature film, what a
major accomplishment!

00:01:29.969 --> 00:01:32.179
[Patricia’s first feature film La Leyenda
Negra premiered in the 2020 Sundance Film

00:01:32.179 --> 00:01:33.179
Festival NEXT program].

00:01:33.179 --> 00:01:39.869
Patricia: But to always keep your feet on
the ground because that was something that

00:01:39.869 --> 00:01:51.569
he would say to us as a screenwriter the secret
to success is self-motivated speculative writing.

00:01:51.569 --> 00:01:56.479
If you just sit around and wait for people
to come to you that’s not going to happen.

00:01:56.479 --> 00:02:02.500
You just need to have your own engine and
you have to keep moving forward regardless.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:10.539
Film Courage: At what point when you hear
that did you approach Mr. Reynoso was it at

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:17.420
Compton High School and ask him to work with
you?

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.530
Did you hear this advice before?

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.989
Patricia: Oh before definitely.

00:02:21.989 --> 00:02:27.719
I think that Richard Walter’s class was
one of the first that I took at UCLA for screenwriting.

00:02:27.719 --> 00:02:37.140
Film Courage: Do you think people in the class
reacted to this advice?

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:41.900
Maybe I’m more of this old mindset where
people expected to be picked for things and

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.560
I don’t think this is the case anymore.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:51.870
Do you think there was a reaction to people
thinking I have to be the one to drum up excitement

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:56.700
or not?

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:02.030
Some people want to be picked and there is
also a feeling of I’ve arrived if I’m

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:03.030
picked.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.330
If I have to push this out then it must not
be good.

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:13.450
I think that’s an old mindset of I need
to keep working at this and I’m the one

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.099
who has to make this happen?

00:03:15.099 --> 00:03:22.239
Patricia: Yes, I think definitely I hear every
young creative in LA say all the time is that

00:03:22.239 --> 00:03:26.379
‘The Hustle is real.’

00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:29.480
You can never get comfortable.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:35.209
Just because things are good now doesn’t
mean they are going to stay that way so you’ve

00:03:35.209 --> 00:03:45.780
really got to just keep working, keep collaborating,
keep making and just stay focused.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:52.349
Because I’ve had very successful filmmakers
say to me that that reason that I’m still

00:03:52.349 --> 00:04:00.510
making films and the colleagues that I worked
with or grew up with are no longer making

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:03.499
films is not because I am more talented than
them.

00:04:03.499 --> 00:04:08.640
It’s just I kept going when they gave up,
that’s the difference.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.000
It’s just persistence and focus and drive
and determination.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.250
That is what is going to keep you moving forward.

